+NOMROM 1.10.4+25 — TorBox file metadata and remote-preparation handling
+
+This follows Windows 1.10.3+24. It retains the Skraper integration, artwork,
+account storage, download integrity, queue controls and signed-update public
+configuration. No publisher signing key or service credentials are included.
+
+Reported behavior
+- Metroid Prime / GameCube Rev 2 and Halo: Combat Evolved / Xbox Rev 2 selections
+  produced FILE_NOT_FOUND in the local TorBox file matcher.
+- Xenoblade v2080 reached remote caching, then NOMROM stopped waiting after its
+  three-minute resolution deadline with CACHING_TIMEOUT. The application did
+  not crash; the user confirmed that only the download stopped.
+
+Provider compatibility changes
+- Recognize TorBox's documented short_name when name is absent or blank.
+  A short filename cannot erase an explicitly conflicting full directory path.
+- Match literal paths first, then try decoding percent-encoded paths once.
+  Preserve literal '+' characters and ambiguous-file rejection.
+- A completed torrent with an empty files list is not evidence that the
+  requested file is absent. Retain the registration and allow another bounded
+  metadata poll instead of immediately returning permanent FILE_NOT_FOUND.
+- Continue to verify torrent hash/ID and file size. Never choose a file by its
+  array position, silently select another revision, or download a whole pack
+  as a substitute for the selected game.
+
+Remote preparation
+When the provider is still preparing at the resolution deadline and native
+torrents are disabled, retain the task using the existing persisted paused
+status. Display Waiting for debrid with a Check again action and release the
+local queue slot. Resume rechecks the original source. Pause/cancel/update
+preparation remain authoritative, and real authentication or network failures
+remain errors. No unlimited background retries or remote deletions are added.
+
+Evidence and limits
+The added provider regression fixtures use public catalog metadata and
+synthetic TorBox responses. They do not contain game data or authenticated
+responses. The delivered 1.10.3 provider fails five of the seven new fixture
+checks; the updated provider passes all seven. Additional queue and widget
+checks cover the remote-preparation flow.
+
+The public catalog identifies Halo (USA) Rev 2 and Xenoblade Chronicles 3D
+(USA) v2080 / Xenoblade (Japan) v2080. It has Metroid Prime (USA) Rev 2, but no
+Metroid Prime 2 Rev 2 entry; no source selection or catalog title was changed
+to resolve that ambiguity.
+
+Original torrent descriptors were fetched without game content. Their raw
+info-dictionary SHA-1 values match the catalog hashes. The requested paths,
+indexes and sizes match the descriptors exactly, so a stale catalog has not
+been demonstrated. The Xenoblade collection contains 124 members totaling
+19,159,807,045 bytes. The GameCube collection is approximately 1.68 TB and the
+Xbox collection 17.54 TB; those are collection sizes, not selected-game sizes.
+
+The user's live TorBox file-list representation remains unverified. A cached
+whole-collection ZIP cannot safely be treated as an individual catalog file.
+The provider corrections are bounded compatibility fixes, not proof that the
+reported Metroid/Halo account response used any particular representation.
+TorBox may need longer than NOMROM's polling window to prepare a collection;
+the app cannot make an uncached or unavailable torrent immediately ready.
+
+Validation is recorded in the separate build evidence. A Windows compile,
+package verification and native smoke run do not replace interactive installer
+or live-service acceptance. Publication still requires the existing publisher
+key and a signed feed; these source changes do not publish an in-app update.
